.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/js-yaml Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
\fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
\fB\fROnline Demo\fP\fP

.PP
This is an implementation of \fRYAML\fP, a human-friendly data serialization language\&. Started as \fRPyYAML\fP port, it was completely rewritten from scratch\&. Now it's very fast, and supports 1\&.2 spec\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.SS "YAML module for node\&.js"
.PP
.nf
npm install js\-yaml
.fi
.PP
.SS "CLI executable"
If you want to inspect your YAML files from CLI, install js-yaml globally:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install \-g js\-yaml
.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage"
.PP
.nf
usage: js\-yaml [\-h] [\-v] [\-c] [\-t] file

Positional arguments:
  file           File with YAML document(s)

Optional arguments:
  \-h, \-\-help     Show this help message and exit\&.
  \-v, \-\-version  Show program's version number and exit\&.
  \-c, \-\-compact  Display errors in compact mode
  \-t, \-\-trace    Show stack trace on error
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
Here we cover the most 'useful' methods\&. If you need advanced details (creating your own tags), see \fRexamples\fP for more info\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
const yaml = require('js\-yaml');
const fs   = require('fs');

// Get document, or throw exception on error
try {
  const doc = yaml\&.load(fs\&.readFileSync('/home/ixti/example\&.yml', 'utf8'));
  console\&.log(doc);
} catch (e) {
  console\&.log(e);
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "load (string [ , options ])"
Parses \fRstring\fP as single YAML document\&. Returns either a plain object, a string, a number, \fRnull\fP or \fRundefined\fP, or throws \fRYAMLException\fP on error\&. By default, does not support regexps, functions and undefined\&.

.PP
options:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRfilename\fP \fI(default: null)\fP - string to be used as a file path in error/warning messages\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRonWarning\fP \fI(default: null)\fP - function to call on warning messages\&. Loader will call this function with an instance of \fRYAMLException\fP for each warning\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRschema\fP \fI(default: \fRDEFAULT_SCHEMA\fP)\fP - specifies a schema to use\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRFAILSAFE_SCHEMA\fP - only strings, arrays and plain objects: http://www.yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html#id2802346
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRJSON_SCHEMA\fP - all JSON-supported types: http://www.yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html#id2803231
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRCORE_SCHEMA\fP - same as \fRJSON_SCHEMA\fP: http://www.yaml.org/spec/1.2/spec.html#id2804923
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRDEFAULT_SCHEMA\fP - all supported YAML types\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRjson\fP \fI(default: false)\fP - compatibility with JSON\&.parse behaviour\&. If true, then duplicate keys in a mapping will override values rather than throwing an error\&.
.PP

.PP
NOTE: This function \fBdoes not\fP understand multi-document sources, it throws exception on those\&.

.PP
NOTE: JS-YAML \fBdoes not\fP support schema-specific tag resolution restrictions\&. So, the JSON schema is not as strictly defined in the YAML specification\&. It allows numbers in any notation, use \fRNull\fP and \fRNULL\fP as \fRnull\fP, etc\&. The core schema also has no such restrictions\&. It allows binary notation for integers\&.
.SS "loadAll (string [, iterator] [, options ])"
Same as \fRload()\fP, but understands multi-document sources\&. Applies \fRiterator\fP to each document if specified, or returns array of documents\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
const yaml = require('js\-yaml');

yaml\&.loadAll(data, function (doc) {
  console\&.log(doc);
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "dump (object [ , options ])"
Serializes \fRobject\fP as a YAML document\&. Uses \fRDEFAULT_SCHEMA\fP, so it will throw an exception if you try to dump regexps or functions\&. However, you can disable exceptions by setting the \fRskipInvalid\fP option to \fRtrue\fP\&.

.PP
options:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRindent\fP \fI(default: 2)\fP - indentation width to use (in spaces)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnoArrayIndent\fP \fI(default: false)\fP - when true, will not add an indentation level to array elements
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRskipInvalid\fP \fI(default: false)\fP - do not throw on invalid types (like function in the safe schema) and skip pairs and single values with such types\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRflowLevel\fP \fI(default: -1)\fP - specifies level of nesting, when to switch from block to flow style for collections\&. -1 means block style everwhere
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyles\fP - "tag" => "style" map\&. Each tag may have own set of styles\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRschema\fP \fI(default: \fRDEFAULT_SCHEMA\fP)\fP specifies a schema to use\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsortKeys\fP \fI(default: \fRfalse\fP)\fP - if \fRtrue\fP, sort keys when dumping YAML\&. If a function, use the function to sort the keys\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlineWidth\fP \fI(default: \fR80\fP)\fP - set max line width\&. Set \fR-1\fP for unlimited width\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnoRefs\fP \fI(default: \fRfalse\fP)\fP - if \fRtrue\fP, don't convert duplicate objects into references
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRnoCompatMode\fP \fI(default: \fRfalse\fP)\fP - if \fRtrue\fP don't try to be compatible with older yaml versions\&. Currently: don't quote "yes", "no" and so on, as required for YAML 1\&.1
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcondenseFlow\fP \fI(default: \fRfalse\fP)\fP - if \fRtrue\fP flow sequences will be condensed, omitting the space between \fRa, b\fP\&. Eg\&. \fR'[a,b]'\fP, and omitting the space between \fRkey: value\fP and quoting the key\&. Eg\&. \fR'{"a":b}'\fP Can be useful when using yaml for pretty URL query params as spaces are %-encoded\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRquotingType\fP \fI(\fR'\fP or \fR"\fP, default: \fR'\fP)\fP - strings will be quoted using this quoting style\&. If you specify single quotes, double quotes will still be used for non-printable characters\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRforceQuotes\fP \fI(default: \fRfalse\fP)\fP - if \fRtrue\fP, all non-key strings will be quoted even if they normally don't need to\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreplacer\fP - callback \fRfunction (key, value)\fP called recursively on each key/value in source object (see \fRreplacer\fP docs for \fRJSON\&.stringify\fP)\&.
.PP

.PP
The following table show availlable styles (e\&.g\&. "canonical", "binary"\&.\&.\&.) available for each tag (\&.e\&.g\&. !!null, !!int \&.\&.\&.)\&. Yaml output is shown on the right side after \fR=>\fP (default setting) or \fR->\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 none
!!null
  "canonical"   \-> "~"
  "lowercase"   => "null"
  "uppercase"   \-> "NULL"
  "camelcase"   \-> "Null"

!!int
  "binary"      \-> "0b1", "0b101010", "0b1110001111010"
  "octal"       \-> "0o1", "0o52", "0o16172"
  "decimal"     => "1", "42", "7290"
  "hexadecimal" \-> "0x1", "0x2A", "0x1C7A"

!!bool
  "lowercase"   => "true", "false"
  "uppercase"   \-> "TRUE", "FALSE"
  "camelcase"   \-> "True", "False"

!!float
  "lowercase"   => "\&.nan", '\&.inf'
  "uppercase"   \-> "\&.NAN", '\&.INF'
  "camelcase"   \-> "\&.NaN", '\&.Inf'
.fi
.PP

.PP
Example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
dump(object, {
  'styles': {
    '!!null': 'canonical' // dump null as ~
  },
  'sortKeys': true        // sort object keys
});
.fi
.PP
.SH "Supported YAML types"
.PP
The list of standard YAML tags and corresponding JavaScript types\&. See also \fRYAML tag discussion\fP and \fRYAML types repository\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
!!null ''                   # null
!!bool 'yes'                # bool
!!int '3\&.\&.\&.'                # number
!!float '3\&.14\&.\&.\&.'           # number
!!binary '\&.\&.\&.base64\&.\&.\&.'     # buffer
!!timestamp 'YYYY\-\&.\&.\&.'      # date
!!omap [ \&.\&.\&. ]              # array of key\-value pairs
!!pairs [ \&.\&.\&. ]             # array or array pairs
!!set { \&.\&.\&. }               # array of objects with given keys and null values
!!str '\&.\&.\&.'                 # string
!!seq [ \&.\&.\&. ]               # array
!!map { \&.\&.\&. }               # object
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBJavaScript-specific tags\fP

.PP
See \fRjs-yaml-js-types\fP for extra types\&.
.SH "Caveats"
.PP
Note, that you use arrays or objects as key in JS-YAML\&. JS does not allow objects or arrays as keys, and stringifies (by calling \fRtoString()\fP method) them at the moment of adding them\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
 yaml
\-\-\-
? [ foo, bar ]
: \- baz
? { foo: bar }
: \- baz
  \- baz
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
 javascript
{ "foo,bar": ["baz"], "[object Object]": ["baz", "baz"] }
.fi
.PP

.PP
Also, reading of properties on implicit block mapping keys is not supported yet\&. So, the following YAML document cannot be loaded\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
 yaml
&anchor foo:
  foo: bar
  *anchor: duplicate key
  baz: bat
  *anchor: duplicate key
.fi
.PP
